From the LXMF parity batch (2026-10-09), same source:

- Link data received through a resource session can be proved by the
  application (PROVE_APP). RNS `Link.py` 945-967; `Packet.py` 338-343.
  Target: `src/endpoint/resource_session.rs`.
- Link data sends can wait for their proof, or for a data answer; the timeout
  is max(6 x RTT, 1 s), the floor standing in for RNS's one-second receipt
  polling. RNS `Packet.py` 338-347, 413-417, 444-475; `Link.py` 81-82,
  378-389, 1192-1196; `Transport.py` 252, 740-763.
  Target: `src/endpoint/link_receipt.rs`.
- Sealing to a destination's advertised ratchet, and opening with retained
  ratchets before the identity key unless ratchets are enforced, as one seam
  used by single packets and by applications. RNS `Destination.py` 596-651;
  `Identity.py` 852-908. Target: `src/endpoint/sealing.rs`.
- Announce app data may be rebuilt at each emission, including path responses.
  RNS `Destination.py` 290-296, 678-686.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/registration.rs`, `src/endpoint/paths.rs`.

From the interface batch (2026-10-09), same source:

- HDLC frames are capped at MTU + 64 (the largest access code); an oversize
  frame is dropped, not truncated. Per-carrier IFAC defaults (16 bytes on
  stream carriers, 8 on serial ones), and `ifac_size` in bits with "" and
  "None" as unset; past 512 bits is refused. RNS `Interfaces/util/HDLC.py`
  62-106; `Transport.py` 1790-1791; `Interfaces/Interface.py` 96;
  `Interfaces/TCPInterface.py` 77, 338-341, 466; `Interfaces/SerialInterface.py`
  53, 85; `Reticulum.py` 884-902, 1041-1042.
  Targets: `src/iface/hdlc.rs`, `src/ifac.rs`, `src/iface/tcp.rs`.
- Per-interface announce ingress control: an interface override (off on
  serial and RNode carriers), known destinations counted but never held, held
  announces queued per interface and released fewest hops first, and announces
  for a destination with a path request in the last 45 s never held. The burst
  detector stays retinue's virtual-arrival model, and held announces have an
  endpoint-wide ceiling RNS lacks. RNS `Interfaces/Interface.py` 70-84, 189-212,
  270-305; `Reticulum.py` 904-927, 1066-1078; `Transport.py` 135, 980-986,
  1098-1101, 1807-1825.
  Targets: `src/announce_admission.rs`, `src/endpoint/announces.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/paths.rs`.
- The destination announce rate: a violation counter with grace and penalty
  (default 3600 s, 5, 0), per-interface overrides, counted for every accepted
  announce that is not a path response and enforced only on relay. The state
  table stays bounded. RNS `Transport.py` 2298-2338; `Interfaces/Interface.py`
  89-92; `Reticulum.py` 651-666, 968-971.
  Targets: `src/announce_admission.rs`, `src/endpoint/announces.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/iface_policy.rs`.
- Interface gravity: the same emission with no more hops, heard on a
  higher-gravity interface, moves the route. Unlike RNS, it queues no
  rebroadcast. RNS `Transport.py` 2225-2251; `Interfaces/Interface.py` 75, 109;
  `Reticulum.py` 881-882, 1035.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/paths.rs`, `src/endpoint/announces.rs`.
- The seven interface modes, their announce rules and path-discovery modes;
  own announces skip access points, and an Endpoint relay takes its next hop
  from the live path at release. Relays to roaming, boundary and internal
  interfaces stay capped, where RNS leaves them uncapped; the Node core treats
  the ingress interface as the next hop. RNS `Interfaces/Interface.py` 45-56;
  `Transport.py` 964-969, 1458-1521, 3426-3433, 3574-3575.
  Targets: `src/iface_mode.rs`, `src/node/`, `src/endpoint/rebroadcast.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/shared.rs`.
- Per-interface announce cap share and configured bitrate, which also set link
  setup and carried-link proof deadlines. An interface with no known airtime
  stays uncapped (RNS caps TCP at an assumed 10 Mbps). RNS `Reticulum.py` 114,
  286-289, 1036; `Interfaces/Interface.py` 117, 391-432; `Transport.py`
  1517-1585, 2059-2062, 3194-3202.
  Targets: `src/rebroadcast.rs`, `src/endpoint/rebroadcast.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/shared.rs`, `src/endpoint/transit.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/link_setup.rs`.
- `outgoing = False` blocks every transmission on the interface, stricter than
  RNS's broadcast-only check. RNS `Reticulum.py` 369-370; `Transport.py` 1449.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/shared.rs`, `src/endpoint/single.rs`.
- Dialed TCP interfaces keep their id and routes across reconnects, drop
  packets while offline, and honour the connect timeout and reconnect limit
  (both ignored upstream); a write error redials where RNS tears down for good,
  and IPv4 is preferred unless `prefer_ipv6`. A 12 s write stall ends a
  connection. RNS `Interfaces/TCPInterface.py` 97-180, 231-303, 314-337,
  344-448; `Interfaces/BackboneInterface.py` 63-69, 476-499, 959-971.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/dial.rs`, `src/endpoint/pump.rs`,
  `src/endpoint/interface.rs`, `src/endpoint/attach.rs`.
- TCP keepalive (5 s idle, 2 s interval, 12 probes; I2P 10/9/5) and user
  timeout (24 s, I2P 45 s, Linux and Android); macOS also gets the interval and
  count. RNS `Interfaces/TCPInterface.py` 83-95, 181-207.
  Target: `src/endpoint/sockopt.rs`.
- Listeners keep accepting after accept errors, and spawned interfaces take the
  listener's mode and interface policy; the IN flag, egress and path-request
  rates and `recursive_prs` are not modelled. RNS
  `Interfaces/TCPInterface.py` 557-650, 675-688;
  `Interfaces/BackboneInterface.py` 658-679, 792-805.
  Target: `src/endpoint/listen.rs`.
- The serial HDLC carrier: 9600 8N1 defaults, a settle delay, a 100 ms
  partial-frame reset, a 5 s reopen under the same interface id, the line
  speed as bitrate, and best-effort DTR/RTS. A macOS pty opens without line
  settings. RNS `Interfaces/SerialInterface.py` 63-230.
  Targets: `src/iface/serial.rs`, `src/iface/serial_port.rs`.
- A sans-io KISS TNC host: startup parameters, the port nibble masked, DATA
  only, and READY flow control. Oversize or badly escaped frames are dropped,
  a 5 s unlock lifts an unanswered lock, and packets wait in the endpoint
  queues rather than the TNC. RNS `Interfaces/KISSInterface.py` 38-58, 84-134,
  178-253, 262-394.
  Targets: `src/iface/kiss.rs`, `src/iface/serial.rs`.
- The station ID beacon for KISS (padded to 15 bytes) and RNode (unpadded, at
  most 32), armed by traffic actually sent; it does not re-arm on its own frame
  (the KISS re-arm is not ported) and is retried when the radio was offline.
  The RNode bridge also takes RNS's LoRa bitrate formula and turns ingress
  control off. RNS `Interfaces/KISSInterface.py` 262-366;
  `Interfaces/RNodeInterface.py` 156-158, 336-347, 698-744, 1145-1149,
  1211-1212.
  Targets: `src/iface/beacon.rs`, `src/iface/tulle.rs`.
- The UDP carrier: one raw packet per datagram, receive bounded at HW_MTU 1064
  plus the access code, 16-byte IFAC by default, the subnet broadcast for a
  device, and a 10 Mbps bitrate guess. It sends from one ephemeral broadcast
  socket (stock opens one per send), drops its own frames heard back within
  2 s, may share a port with SO_REUSEADDR, and a forward-only carrier is
  online (stock leaves it offline). RNS `Interfaces/UDPInterface.py` 40-151;
  `Interfaces/util/netinfo.py` 64-71; `Transport.py` 1790-1791.
  Targets: `src/iface/udp.rs`, `src/iface/netinfo.rs`.
- AutoInterface addressing: the discovery group, the peering token over the
  RFC 5952 link-local text with the KAME scope cleared (also when the interface
  id holds the longest zero run, which stock's pattern misses), and the
  multi-interface duplicate ring. RNS `Interfaces/AutoInterface.py` 47-59,
  80-85, 164-213, 369-371, 490-513; `Interfaces/util/netinfo.py` 157-171.
  Target: `src/auto.rs`.
- The AutoInterface carrier: interface selection with the Darwin and Android
  skip lists and a strict UP/MULTICAST filter, discovery, reverse peering and
  data sockets, peer timeouts, carrier echo tracking and one interface per
  peer. Unlike stock: peers are keyed by address and interface index and capped
  at 64 per interface; tokens go from the bound data socket; a new peer gets an
  immediate unicast token; a link-local change rebinds all three sockets, and
  the adopted address is kept while present; no adoptable interface is an
  error. RNS `Interfaces/AutoInterface.py` 44-73, 103-356, 358-482, 485-520,
  526-614, 633-700; `Reticulum.py` 1114-1116.
  Target: `src/iface/auto/`.

## Prns

Reference withdrawn September 26 over unresolved provenance concerns. Existing
attribution and license text remain for retained material; they do not verify
upstream authorship. See the repository's Prns donor ledger.

`src/artifact.rs` and `src/msgpack.rs` implement the RNS signed-artifact
envelope (`.rsg` / `.rsm`). Its layout was read from Prns
(<https://github.com/KenAKAFrosty/Prns>), a Rust Reticulum
implementation, at commit `72b6b30d27cac910ce20d370e1dc711fe9b95955` (v0.3.4).
Prns is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; Retinue elects **MIT**.

The Rust in those two files was written for this crate and shares no text with
Prns. What was taken is the structure of the envelope: which fields exist, what
they are named, that the metadata map opens with `signer` and `pubkey`, and
that the signature covers the encoded envelope rather than the message. That is
a description of an RNS wire format, and the vectors proving Retinue reproduces
it were captured independently by running RNS 1.4.2's own `rnid` executable
(`oracle/capture_signed_artifact.py`).

The historical test quoted Prns's 224-byte published constant. That comparison
was retired on September 26; current captures use project-selected inputs with
RNS 1.5.2. The original captures remain in the receipt archive and Git history.
